[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "A41"]
[Annotator "Littlewood, J"]
[PlyCount "67"]
[EventDate "1964.09.19"]
[EventType "team-match"]
[EventRounds "2"]
[EventCountry "NED"]
[SourceTitle "BritBase"]
[Source "John Saunders"]
[SourceDate "1997.08.31"]
[SourceVersion "1"]
[SourceVersionDate "1997.08.31"]
[SourceQuality "1"]
[WhiteTeam "Great Britain"]
[BlackTeam "Netherlands"]
[WhiteTeamCountry "GBR"]
[BlackTeamCountry "NED"]

1. d4 d6 {Inviting me to play 2 e4, so that he can obtain his pet variation of the Robatsch Defence. This time I politely declined.} 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. Nc3 Bg4 {A move I have never seen before in this position, although it must certainly have been played. Its thematic idea is to strengthen Black’s grip on the squares d4 and e5. Indeed, after 5 e4 Nc6 6 Be3 e5 Black realizes his aim.} 5. e3 $1 {A quiet but effective move. I refuse to give up the struggle for the black squares and leave [the] bishop looking a little embarrassed on g4; sooner or later it must retreat, as to take my Knight allows me the two bishops without any compensation.} Nc6 6. Be2 e5 7. dxe5 $1 {All part of my plan.} dxe5 ({If now} 7... Nxe5 8. Nxe5 Bxe2 9. Nxf7 $1 {wins a pawn.}) 8. Qb3 b6 $2 ({It is not too fanciful to attribute the loss of the game to this move. It weakens the white squares on Black’s Queen’s side and gives my attack something to "bite" on. Better was} 8... Nge7 9. O-O Qc8 {, with a difficult but playable defence.}) 9. c5 $1 {Immediately pouncing on the weakness as well as exploiting Black’s "hanging" Queen’s Bishop.} Be6 ({If now} 9... Nge7 10. Ng5 {wins}) ({whilst if} 9... bxc5 10. Bb5 Bd7 11. Ne4 $1 Nge7 12. Nfg5 O-O 13. Nxf7 $1 Rxf7 14. Ng5 Be8 15. Bc4 {reveals how easily Black’s position could collapse. His next few moves are forced.}) 10. Qa4 Nge7 11. O-O O-O 12. Rd1 Bd7 $1 ({And not} 12... Qe8 13. Ng5 Bd7 14. Qh4 h6 15. Nge4 f5 16. Bc4+ Kh8 17. Ng5 $1 {, with great pressure.}) 13. Qa3 $1 {A move which shows how my chess style has matured. It would be positional suicide to try for a King’s-side attack now, as that is where Black’s sole strength lies. It is now time to develop my remaining pieces, after which my Queen’s-side offensive can begin in earnest.} Qe8 14. Bd2 h6 15. Rac1 ({Not} 15. Nb5 Rc8 16. Nxa7 Ra8 {, etc}) ({nor} 15. b4 a5 $1 16. b5 Nb4 17. c6 Be6 {when it is Black who has the winning chances.}) 15... f5 ({Black should play first the preparatory} 15... Kh7 {, but understandably wishes to flex his muscles. At the same time he sets a "trap" which I walk into with my eyes wide open . . .}) 16. Nb5 $1 Qc8 (16... Rc8 {would now lose to} 17. Nxa7 Ra8 18. Qb3+) 17. Bc3 $1 a6 18. Nd6 $3 ({(Black had been] hoping for} 18. Qb3+ $2 Be6 19. Bc4 Bxc4 20. Qxc4+ Kh8 21. Na3 $4 b5 22. Qb3 b4 23. Bxb4 Rb8 {winning, but now comes the logical culmination of White’s build up on the Queen’s side. A purely positional sacrifice, as in the main line I gain no material. The main object is to open up lines for all my pieces and subsequently take advantage of Black’s weakened King’s side.}) 18... cxd6 ({The Knight must be taken, as} 18... Qb8 19. Qb3+ Kh7 20. Nf7 {wins at least the e-pawn.}) 19. cxd6 Re8 (19... Qe8 20. dxe7 Nxe7 21. Bc4+ {and 22 Nxe5 gives White an overwhelming position.}) 20. dxe7 ({Tempting now is an immediate} 20. Nxe5 Bxe5 ({but Black plays instead} 20... Nxe5 21. Bxe5 Bxe5 $1 22. dxe7 $1 (22. Rxc8 Nxc8 $1) 22... Bc7 $1 {when I could not see a clear winning line at the time. Later analysis has convinced me that even in this variation, I have a tremen­dous attack after} 23. Qb3+ $1 Kh7 (23... Be6 24. Bc4 Rxe7 25. Bxe6+ Rxe6 26. Rxc7 $1 {wins}) 24. Qf7+ Kh8 25. Bc4 $3 {, etc., but I can hardly be blamed for failing to see all this in the time available, especially as the simpler alternative gives me a winning position.}) 21. Bxe5 Nxe5 22. dxe7 Qb7 23. Qd6 $1) 20... Rxe7 21. Nxe5 $1 {The point.} Rxe5 ({If now} 21... Bxe5 22. Bxe5 Rxe5 {, then} 23. Qd6 $1 {wins.}) 22. Bxe5 Bxe5 23. Qb3+ Kg7 24. Qd5 Ra7 25. Rxc6 Bxh2+ 26. Kxh2 Bxc6 {The end of the combination reveals Black’s weakened King’s side and passively placed pieces as the decisive factors.} 27. Qe5+ ({I could now win a pawn by} 27. Qd4+ Kh7 28. Qxb6 {but after} Rb7 $1 29. Qxa6 Qc7+ 30. f4 Rxb2 {Black would be granted a new lease of life. An attack on the black King is called for.}) 27... Kh7 28. Rd6 b5 29. f4 $1 ({A necessary precaution, as} 29. Qf6 Be8 30. Rd8 {fails to} Qc7+ 31. f4 Qe7 $1 {I now threaten 30 Qf6.}) 29... Be8 ({If} 29... Rd7 30. Qe6 $1) ({or if} 29... Qe8 30. Re6 $1 {The doctor is now in great time-trouble, so I give the remaining moves without comment.}) 30. Bf3 Bf7 31. Qd4 Qc7 32. Rd8 g5 33. Qf6 Qe7 34. Rh8# {Sweet revenge indeed for the severe defeat which Barendregt inflicted on me at Birmingham last year. Source: BCM, November 1964, p342 (n.b. the moves are misnumbered in BCM, from move 5 onwards)} 1-0

